meld, reducing chances for concealed patterns. If you Pass, you keep hand closed except the one exposed pung, and you can try to draw useful tiles. Given the hand is still fragmented, maybe it's better to Pass and try to improve shape, as Chi would commit to a specific sequence and reduce flexibility.\n\nAlso, look at discards: Many terminals and honors have been discarded. Tiles like T4, T7, B1, B4 are still available in decent numbers (T4:3, T7:3, B1:4, B4:4). So chances to complete chows are okay.\n\nReason: Passing allows you to maintain a more flexible hand with multiple options for chows and keep the pair. Chi would fix one meld but leave a less coherent remaining set. Since you are not close to a high-value hand and need to build more melds, it's better to avoid exposing another meld prematurely and wait for a better draw to improve the hand structure.\n\nAnswer: Pass"}
